## 3. React Crash Course (just enough)

### Components
A **component** is a reusable piece of UI. In React, every component is just a **JavaScript function that returns JSX** (HTML-like syntax):

```jsx
// This is a React component
const Greeting = ({ name }) => {
  return <h1>Hello, {name}!</h1>;
};
```

### JSX
JSX looks like HTML but is actually JavaScript. Key differences from HTML:
- Use `className` instead of `class`
- Use `{}` to embed JavaScript expressions: `<p>{2 + 2}</p>` renders "4"
- Self-closing tags are required: `<img />` not `<img>`

### Hooks (useState, useEffect, useContext)

Hooks are special functions that let components "hook into" React features:

| Hook | Purpose | Example |
|---|---|---|
| `useState` | Store & update data within a component | `const [count, setCount] = useState(0)` |
| `useEffect` | Run code when the component loads or when some data changes | Fetch data from API on page load |
| `useContext` | Access data shared across many components without passing props | Access logged-in user info from anywhere |
| `useRef` | Hold a reference to a DOM element or a mutable value | Reference to the WebSocket client |
| `useNavigate` | Navigate to a different page programmatically | `navigate('/login')` after logout |
| `useParams` | Read URL parameters | `useParams()` gives `{ id: "5" }` for `/property/5` |

### Props
Data passed **from parent to child** component:
```jsx
// Parent passes "name" prop
<Greeting name="Akash" />

// Child receives it
const Greeting = ({ name }) => <h1>Hello, {name}!</h1>;
```

### Conditional Rendering
Show different things based on conditions:
```jsx
{isLoggedIn ? <LogoutButton /> : <LoginButton />}
```

### Rendering Lists
Use `.map()` to render an array of items:
```jsx
{items.map(item => <Card key={item.id} title={item.title} />)}
```

